#EXTM3U
# The second guard on dl-40's collapse, and the one that makes it dangerous: two
# renditions of the same rung that differ only in H.264 profile.
#
# Also not the reported video's shape. Its profiles did differ — Baseline, Main
# and High across the ladder — but only *between* rungs, which the resolution
# column already separates. Within a rung its two entries were byte-identical on
# CODECS. This fixture is the case that would break, not the case that was
# reported.
#
# ffmpeg 6.1.1 wrote every line below (hlsenc, unedited):
#
#   ffmpeg -f lavfi -i "testsrc2=size=1280x720:rate=25:duration=6" \
#          -f lavfi -i "sine=frequency=440:sample_rate=48000:duration=6" \
#          -filter_complex "[0:v]split=3[a][b][c];[a]scale=1280:720[v0];\
#            [b]scale=1280:720[v1];[c]scale=640:360[v2]" \
#          -map "[v0]" -map 1:a -map "[v1]" -map 1:a -map "[v2]" -map 1:a \
#          -c:a aac -b:a 96k -pix_fmt yuv420p \
#          -c:v:0 libx264 -profile:v:0 high -b:v:0 1200k \
#          -c:v:1 libx264 -profile:v:1 baseline -b:v:1 1200k \
#          -c:v:2 libx264 -profile:v:2 high -b:v:2 600k \
#          -var_stream_map "v:0,a:0 v:1,a:1 v:2,a:2" \
#          -f hls -hls_time 2 -hls_playlist_type vod \
#          -hls_segment_filename "%v/seg%d.ts" -master_pl_name master.m3u8 \
#          "%v/index.m3u8"
#
# A ladder carrying a Baseline rendition beside a High one, for devices that
# cannot decode the better profile, is ordinary. `shortCodec` renders both as
# `H.264` by design and the picker shows no other column that separates them, so
# the two rows read identically — and a collapse that only checked the rendered
# columns would throw one of them away. That is a real rendition and a real
# choice, unlike a duplicated CDN path, which is why the collapse has to look at
# the declared codec first.
#
# The two BANDWIDTH values are equal here because both rungs were given the same
# target and hlsenc computes the attribute from the targets, not from the
# segments (see hls-master-redundant-cdns.m3u8). The collision on screen does not
# depend on that: `formatBitrate` rounds to one decimal above 1 Mbps, so two
# genuinely different bitrates land on the same "1.4 Mbps" just as easily.
#EXT-X-VERSION:3
#EXT-X-STREAM-INF:BANDWIDTH=1425600,RESOLUTION=1280x720,CODECS="avc1.64001f,mp4a.40.2"
0/index.m3u8

#EXT-X-STREAM-INF:BANDWIDTH=1425600,RESOLUTION=1280x720,CODECS="avc1.42c01f,mp4a.40.2"
1/index.m3u8

#EXT-X-STREAM-INF:BANDWIDTH=765600,RESOLUTION=640x360,CODECS="avc1.64001e,mp4a.40.2"
2/index.m3u8
